● Good questions — and the repo's actual state gives a clear, concrete answer.
  Let me lay it out.

  Two independent pipelines

  Your site is served by two separate mechanisms that don't talk to each other:

  ┌─────────┬───────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
  │         │   GitHub Pages (live site)    │        GitHub Actions         │
  │         │                               │      (jekyll-build.yml)       │
  ├─────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
  │         │ Every push to main            │ Push to                       │
  │ Trigger │ (automatic, built-in)         │ jekyll-minimal-mistakes +     │
  │         │                               │ manual workflow_dispatch      │
  ├─────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
  │         │ Legacy Jekyll build           │ bundle exec jekyll build      │
  │ Builds  │ (github-pages gem +           │ --destination _site           │
  │         │ remote_theme)                 │                               │
  ├─────────┼───────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┤
  │         │ Deploys to                    │ Uploads _site as a            │
  │ Output  │ https://tthtlc.github.io/     │ downloadable artifact — no    │
  │         │                               │ deploy                        │
  └─────────┴───────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

  I confirmed the Pages setup via the API: "build_type": "legacy", "source": { 
  "branch": "main", "path": "/" }. So main is the single source of truth for the
  live blog, built and deployed by GitHub Pages itself.

  1. Implication of the workflow triggering on jekyll-minimal-mistakes

  Normal content updates — including the commit I just pushed — land on main, so
  they do not trigger this CI at all. The workflow is effectively dormant: it
  only fires if someone pushes to that one specific branch (or clicks "Run
  workflow" manually). You get no CI validation or artifact for main pushes.

  The run history confirms it: it has run exactly three times, all on
  2026-09-05, all from pushes to jekyll-minimal-mistakes:

  success   Pin github-pages gem to ~> 232      (48s)
  failure   Pin Ruby 3.3 in build workflow      (45s)
  failure   Add GitHub Actions workflow ...     (8s)

  2. Purpose of the jekyll-minimal-mistakes branch

  It's a legacy working branch — the tip commit is literally "Migrate site to 
  Jekyll with Minimal Mistakes theme". The story is:

  1. The site was migrated to Jekyll/Minimal Mistakes on this branch.
  2. The GitHub Actions build workflow was then added and iterated on here
     (workflow file → pin Ruby → pin github-pages gem) until the build passed.
  3. The whole thing was merged into main.

  Right now it's a strict ancestor of main: every commit in it already exists in
  main, and main is 21 commits ahead. So it serves no live purpose — it's
  redundant, stale history (it predates all your recent zonohedra/surface work).
  It can be safely deleted; it's fully merged. (Side note: there's also a
  leftover Claude Code worktree jekyll-build-wf at the same tip — that looks
  like an isolated session where this build workflow was experimented on.)

  3. What happened after a CI build of this branch

  The workflow does four things: checkout → set up Ruby 3.3 → jekyll build →
  upload _site as the "site" artifact. So a successful run produces a
  downloadable compiled copy of the site in the Actions tab (retained ~90 days
  by default), which a human can inspect — and nothing more. It does not publish
  anything to your blog. The live site is unaffected because GitHub Pages
  builds main separately.

  Bottom line / what I'd suggest

  The workflow is currently a build-validation script pointed at a dead branch,
  and it doesn't deploy. Two small changes would make it useful again:

  - Change on.push.branches to main so every push gets a CI build check.
  - If you want it to actually deploy, either leave Pages as-is (legacy build on
    main already deploys) or migrate to the modern "GitHub Pages (Actions)"
    flow (actions/deploy-pages) for a single unified pipeline.

  Want me to point the workflow at main (and optionally add a real deploy job)?

